Great! You can use the API from Alpha Vantage to get the current exchange rate between USD and silver. Here's an example of how you could use the API in your `content.js` file:
```
// API key from Alpha Vantage
var apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY_HERE';
// Get the exchange rate from USD to silver
var exchangeRate;
fetch('https://www.alphavantage.co/query?function=CURRENCY_EXCHANGE_RATE&from_currency=USD&to_currency=XAG&apikey=' + apiKey)
    .then(function(response) {
        return response.json();
    })
    .then(function(data) {
        exchangeRate = data['Realtime Currency Exchange Rate']['5. Exchange Rate'];
        console.log(exchangeRate);
        // Get all elements with the class "price"
        var prices = document.getElementsByClassName("price");
        // Loop through all the elements and replace the text with the converted price
        for (var i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
            var price = parseFloat(prices[i].innerText.replace(/[^0-9\.]+/g,""));
            var silverPrice = price * exchangeRate;
            prices[i].innerText = silverPrice + " oz";
        }
    });
```
This code first uses the `fetch()` function to call the Alpha Vantage API and get the current exchange rate between USD and silver. The API key is passed as a parameter in the URL. The `.then(function(response) { return response.json(); })` is to parse the response as a JSON object.
The `exchangeRate` variable is then updated with the value from the API response, and the rest of the code that modifies the prices on the page is run inside the second `.then()` function.
You'll need to replace `'YOUR_API_KEY_HERE'` with your actual API key.
It's also important to note that this is just an example, and you may need to adjust the code to match the specific format of the data returned by the Alpha Vantage API, as well as the specific HTML structure of the website you're trying to convert prices on.

