To create a Google Chrome extension, you will need to have some knowledge of web development, specifically JavaScript and HTML. You can use JavaScript to access the page's DOM (Document Object Model) and modify the prices on the page. The extension will need to use the chrome.tabs API to access the current tab, and use JavaScript to find and replace the prices with their silver equivalent.
1. Create a new folder for your extension and give it a name.
2. Create a manifest.json file in the folder and add the following information to it:
```
{
    "manifest_version": 2,
    "name": "Currency Converter",
    "description": "Convert prices from USD to silver",
    "version": "1.0",
    "permissions": [
        "activeTab"
    ],
    "content_scripts": [{
        "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
        "js": ["content.js"]
    }]
}
```
3. Create a content.js file in the same folder, this is where the JavaScript code to access the page's DOM and modify the prices will go.
4. Use chrome.tabs API to access the current tab's DOM and use JavaScript to find and replace the prices with their silver equivalent.
5. Go to chrome://extensions/ in your browser, enable developer mode and load the extension by clicking on "Load unpacked" and select the folder that you created for your extension.
This is a basic outline of how you can create a Chrome extension to convert prices from USD to silver. Keep in mind that this is a complex task and will require a good understanding of web development, JavaScript, and the Chrome Extension API.", "User: show me what the JS should look like?", "Assistant: Here's an example of what the JavaScript code in your `content.js` file could look like:
```
// Get all elements with the class "price"
var prices = document.getElementsByClassName("price");
// Get the exchange rate from USD to silver
var exchangeRate = 0.01; // this is just an example, you'll need to find a way to get the current exchange rate
// Loop through all the elements and replace the text with the converted price
for (var i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
    var price = parseFloat(prices[i].innerText.replace(/[^0-9\.]+/g,""));
    var silverPrice = price * exchangeRate;
    prices[i].innerText = silverPrice + " oz";
}
```
This code first uses `document.getElementsByClassName("price")` to get all elements on the page that have the class "price". It then loops through each element, uses `parseFloat` to get the numerical value of the price, multiplies it by the exchange rate to convert it to silver, and then replaces the text of the element with the converted price.
You could also use `querySelectorAll` to select elements by CSS selectors, such as `'span.price'`, `'span[data-price]'`, or `'div#product-price'`
```
var prices = document.querySelectorAll('span.price');
```
It's important to note that this is just an example and you may need to adjust the code to match the specific HTML structure of the website you're trying to convert prices on.
Keep in mind that this example would require a way to get the current exchange rate, so you'll need to find a way to get that and update the exchangeRate variable accordingly."]

