extra_reference:
You are correct that the tangent function is undefined at 90 and 270 degrees (and their multiples), which can cause issues with the calculation of the intercept point between two rays when the angle of one or both of the rays is one of these values.
To avoid this problem, we can use the slope-intercept form of a line, which is y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. This form works for all angles, including vertical lines.
Here's an updated version of the code that uses the slope-intercept form instead of the tangent function:
```javascript
function calculateInterceptPoint(ray1Point, ray1Angle, ray2Point, ray2Angle) {
  const ray1Slope = Math.cos(ray1Angle) / Math.sin(ray1Angle);
  const ray2Slope = Math.cos(ray2Angle) / Math.sin(ray2Angle);
  // check for parallel or divergent rays
  if (ray1Slope === ray2Slope || (ray1Slope > 0 && ray2Slope > 0) || (ray1Slope < 0 && ray2Slope < 0)) {
    return null;
  }
  const ray1YIntercept = ray1Point.y - ray1Slope * ray1Point.x;
  const ray2YIntercept = ray2Point.y - ray2Slope * ray2Point.x;
  const xIntercept = (ray2YIntercept - ray1YIntercept) / (ray1Slope - ray2Slope);
  const yIntercept = ray1Slope * xIntercept + ray1YIntercept;
  return { x: xIntercept, y: yIntercept };
}
```
This version of the code calculates the slope of each ray using the formula `slope = cos(angle) / sin(angle)`, which avoids the problem of undefined values for the tangent function. It also uses the y-intercept of each ray to calculate the intercept point.

